course: arts
lesson: 3
title: Practising when you would rather not
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Practising is how your fingers and brain learn new moves, but not all practice is equal: twenty minutes fixing one hard bit can do more than an hour of playing what you already know.
explain: Picture Darya, who trips at bar 12 and goes back to the very start each time: she plays the easy bars fifty times and the hard bar only once, so the hard bar stays hard. Pulling out that one bar and playing it slowly fixes it.
explain: Slow is the secret: if you practise fast with mistakes, your fingers learn the mistakes; play it slowly and right first, and speed comes later.
explain: Nearly everyone is bad for the first weeks and finds practice boring sometimes, even professionals; and pain is a warning sign, so take breaks and stop if it hurts.
explain: Different places say it their own way: Gen Z "it's giving prison" means it feels like prison, Singaporeans say "lah" for emphasis and "can or not" for "is it possible", Australians say "arvo" for afternoon, and in Ireland "wojus" means terrible.
fact: Focused practice means practising with full attention on one goal; watching the clock while repeating easy parts changes very little.
fact: Most musicians say the dull part of practice is where skills grow; short sessions, small goals and a fun piece at the end help.
fact: Deliberate practice means working on purpose on the parts you cannot do yet, slowly and carefully.
fact: The "10,000 hours" idea was made famous by the writer Malcolm Gladwell, based on research by the psychologist Anders Ericsson.
fact: Ericsson himself said 10,000 was not a magic rule and that the quality of practice mattered more than the count.
fact: Playing slowly first lets fingers learn the right moves; fast practice full of mistakes trains the mistakes.
fact: Neighbours need rest and quiet too; many electric keyboards have a headphone socket for quiet practice.
fact: People start with different strengths, but research suggests practice explains a lot of how good people become.
fact: To isolate a part means to practise one small bit on its own, then join it back to the bars around it.
fact: Many teachers suggest giving a new instrument at least a full term before deciding, and recording yourself shows your progress.
fact: Researchers have reported that mental practice, imagining playing, can help a bit, mostly when mixed with real practice.
fact: Playing too long without breaks can cause overuse injuries, and pushing through pain can make them worse.
exercise: Maksym repeats an easy line while watching a 20-minute timer. What would help more? | Focusing on one hard bit until it is fixed | Setting a longer timer and doing exactly the same | Playing the easy line faster each time | Practising only when Mum checks
exercise: Aiden says practice is boring "fr fr" (for real). What is true? | Almost everyone finds practice boring sometimes, even professionals | Boring practice proves he was never meant to play music at all, fr | Only beginners ever find practice boring | Practice should never feel boring at all
exercise: Sophie plays her favourite easy bit ten times and skips the scary page. What is the problem? | The hard page stays hard because it never gets practised | She is wearing out the easy bit | Easy bits should never be played twice | Nothing, playing easy bits makes you better at everything
exercise: Rohan says you need exactly 10,000 hours, so it's too late to start. What is true? | Even the researcher said the number was not a magic rule | He is right, anything under 10,000 hours is useless | 10,000 hours is a law passed by music schools | You need 10,000 hours before you can play even one proper note
exercise: Who made the "10,000 hours" idea famous? | The writer Malcolm Gladwell | The composer Bach | A famous drummer from Liverpool | The inventor of the piano
exercise: Mei wants to play fast "can or not" (is it OK). What is wise? | Play it slowly and right first, then speed up | Always practise at full speed, so you get fast | Speed and accuracy have nothing to do with each other | Play fast and just fix the mistakes in the concert
exercise: Charlie plays keyboard at half six on a Sunday morning. What is a fair fix? | Practise at fair times, or use headphones | Play louder so the neighbour gets used to it | Neighbours have no right to quiet if it is your home | Move the keyboard nearer to the open window
exercise: Grandad says, "Either you've got it or you haven't." What does research suggest? | Practice explains a lot of how good people become | Musical skill is fixed from birth and never changes | Only grandads can tell who is talented | Talent matters and practice does not
exercise: Darya keeps tripping at bar 12 and restarting from the top. What should she do? | Pull out bar 12 and practise it alone, slowly | Keep starting from the top until it works | Skip bar 12 in every performance | Play the whole piece much faster to get past bar 12 before it trips her
exercise: Ciara quits the tin whistle after one week. What do many teachers suggest? | Give it at least a full term before deciding | Quit after one week if it sounds bad | Switch instruments every few days | Only keep going if it sounds good on day one
exercise: Kenji wiggles his fingers on the bus. What is he doing? | Mental practice, which helps a bit alongside real practice | Something that fully replaces real practice on a real instrument | A habit that makes his playing worse | A warm-up that only works for drummers
exercise: Ivy's wrist aches after three hours of guitar. What should she do? | Take breaks, and stop and tell an adult if it hurts | Push through the ache, because no pain means no gain | Practise even longer to toughen her wrist | Switch to playing with her other hand
exercise: What does "isolate" mean in practice? | Practise one small part on its own | Practise alone in a quiet locked room | Play every part of the piece at once | Stop practising for a while to rest
exercise: Why does recording yourself help? | It shows how much you have improved | It makes the music sound louder and better | It replaces practising | It stops all mistakes
test: Your mate goes, "long practice = better, whatever you do in it, innit." Is he right? | No, time without attention changes very little | yes, the clock is all that matters, the rest is just extra | Yes, but only after 10,000 hours | No, practice never helps anyone
test: What would happen if you always practised a piece fast with lots of mistakes? | Your fingers would learn the mistakes | The mistakes would vanish by themselves | You would play perfectly at the concert | The piece would simply get easier on its own
test: Grandad in Yorkshire says to Ella, "you haven't got it, bless yer" ("bless yer" softens a hard comment). What is the kindest true reply? | A slow start is just a start, and practice builds skill | He is right, she should stop at once and try a sport instead | Being blessed makes you play better | Only people born musical should practise
test: A cartoon hero plays a piece once, badly, then wins a big contest the next day. What is wrong with this? | Real skill needs weeks of practice, not one go | Nothing, that is exactly how all real musicians learn their pieces | Contests only let in beginners | Heroes in cartoons never play music
test: Ciara says she's "wojus" (terrible) after a week. What is most likely? | Nearly everyone is bad in the first weeks | She will be bad at it forever, whatever she does | The tin whistle she was given must be broken | One week is the time it takes to master music
test: Kenji's sister says imagining playing is "the same as real practice". Which is true? | It helps a little, but works best alongside real playing | It is exactly the same as real practice, just quieter and easier | It does nothing at all | It is better than touching a real instrument
test: Neighbour in Melbourne says, "give it a rest till the arvo" ("arvo" means afternoon). What does he want? | Charlie to wait until the afternoon to play | Charlie to play louder in the morning | Charlie to give the keyboard away | Charlie to practise for longer
test: Which plan uses 20 minutes best? | Fix one tricky bar slowly, then play a fun piece | Play the easiest piece over and over | Watch the clock and play whatever comes | Play the whole book through once at top speed
test: Ivy says, "No pain no gain, eh." What should she know? | Pain from playing is a warning, not a reward | Pain means she is practising well | Pain always goes away by itself if she keeps playing for longer | Only beginners get hurt from playing
test: Your uncle reckons that if practice is boring, you're "just not meant for music". Is he right? | No, even professionals find practice boring sometimes | Yes, real musicians always love every minute of their practising | Yes, boredom shows you have no talent | No, practice is only boring for adults
test: What would happen if Darya practised bar 12 slowly ten times, then joined it to the bars around it? | Bar 12 would most likely stop tripping her up | She would forget the rest of the piece | Bar 12 would get even harder | Nothing, slow practice never helps
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of a practice method in which a performer recommences the entire piece upon each error. | The difficult passage receives the least repetition of any passage | The difficult passage receives an excess of repetition | The method causes the instrument to deteriorate | The method has no deficiency whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called ten-thousand-hour rule. | The researcher upon whose work it was based stated that the figure was not a fixed rule | The figure is a precise threshold below which no competence whatsoever is ever attained | The rule was enacted by statute in several countries | The rule was formulated by Johann Sebastian Bach
exam: Determine the most probable consequence of habitual rapid practice accompanied by frequent errors. | The errors are themselves learned by the fingers | The errors are spontaneously eliminated | The tempo of the piece is permanently reduced | The performer acquires flawless accuracy
exam: Ascertain the appropriate response of a performer who experiences pain in the course of prolonged practice. | The cessation of playing, the taking of rest and the informing of an adult | The continuation of playing in order to strengthen the affected part | The doubling of the duration of practice | The disregarding of the pain as a sign of diligence
